## Summary

[GHSA-fp43-vj7g-pg92](https://github.com/omnifaces/omnifaces/security/advisories/GHSA-fp43-vj7g-pg92) (fixed 5.4.2) bound session- and view-scoped **WebSocket** (`o:socket`) push channels to the HTTP session that registered them, so a leaked channel ID can no longer be connected to from a foreign or absent HTTP session. The fix ([`d5cae243c4`](https://github.com/omnifaces/omnifaces/commit/d5cae243c4692555efaa4ba774e0f8f60e3f4db5)) touched only the `Socket*` classes.

The parallel **SSE** (`o:sse`, added in 5.2) push transport was left unchanged. `SseEndpoint.doGet` accepts a connection whenever the presented channel ID is in the application-wide registry, without ever checking that the caller's HTTP session owns it. A leaked session/view-scoped SSE channel ID can therefore be replayed from any (or no) HTTP session to receive the messages pushed to it — exactly the behaviour the advisory removed for WebSocket, still present on SSE at the latest release.

## Root Cause

Both push transports share the same base classes. A session- or view-scoped channel ID is minted as `channel + "?" + UUID.randomUUID()` and registered only in an application-scoped, session-agnostic registry: [`PushChannelManager#L123`](https://github.com/omnifaces/omnifaces/blob/7cd869c039c22c8a107547ec118717cd9b68305b/src/main/java/org/omnifaces/cdi/push/PushChannelManager.java#L123) mints it and [`#L136`](https://github.com/omnifaces/omnifaces/blob/7cd869c039c22c8a107547ec118717cd9b68305b/src/main/java/org/omnifaces/cdi/push/PushChannelManager.java#L136) calls `getPushSessions().register(channelId)`; the registry's membership test is [`PushSessionManager#L137-L139`](https://github.com/omnifaces/omnifaces/blob/7cd869c039c22c8a107547ec118717cd9b68305b/src/main/java/org/omnifaces/cdi/push/PushSessionManager.java#L137-L139):

```java
protected boolean isRegistered(String channelId) {
    return sessions.containsKey(channelId); // keyed by channelId only — no HTTP-session dimension
}
```

**WebSocket (fixed).** `SocketChannelManager.register` additionally records session/view channel IDs on the HTTP session, and the handshake refuses anything not owned by the caller's session, at [`SocketEndpoint.java#L133-L142`](https://github.com/omnifaces/omnifaces/blob/7cd869c039c22c8a107547ec118717cd9b68305b/src/main/java/org/omnifaces/cdi/push/SocketEndpoint.java#L133-L142):

```java
if (
    !SocketChannelManager.isApplicationScopedChannelId(channelId)
        && !SocketChannelManager.isChannelIdRegisteredInSession((HttpSession) request.getHttpSession(), channelId)
) {
    throw new IllegalStateException(ERROR_UNAUTHORIZED_CHANNEL);
}
```

**SSE (unfixed).** `SseChannelManager` extends the base `register()` unchanged (no HTTP-session registration), and the endpoint's sole accept gate is the global `isRegistered` check, at [`SseEndpoint.java#L59-L66`](https://github.com/omnifaces/omnifaces/blob/7cd869c039c22c8a107547ec118717cd9b68305b/src/main/java/org/omnifaces/cdi/push/SseEndpoint.java#L59-L66):

```java
var channel = getChannel(request);
var channelId = channel + "?" + request.getQueryString();
var sseSessions = Beans.getReference(SseSessionManager.class);

if (channel == null || !sseSessions.isRegistered(channelId)) {
    response.getOutputStream().write(SSE_NOT_FOUND_EVENT);
    return;
}
// ...accepts: startAsync() and streams events for this channel
```

`doGet` never calls `request.getSession(...)`; no `Sse*` class consults the HTTP session for the accept decision. So for a session/view-scoped channel ID the fix intends to bind, WebSocket refuses a foreign/absent session while SSE accepts it. (Application-scoped channels are unbound by design on both transports — not at issue here.)

Related, same root cause: the idle-timeout and max-sessions-per-channel bounds the fix added to `SocketEndpoint` / `SocketSessionManager` were also not ported to SSE — `SseEndpoint.doGet` sets `asyncContext.setTimeout(0)` and `SseSessionManager` inherits the unbounded `getMaxSessionsPerChannel()`.

## Steps to Reproduce

The PoC drives the real `SseEndpoint.doGet` from omnifaces 5.4.2 and shows it accepts a leaked session-scoped channel ID from a request carrying no owning HTTP session (and never consults the session), while the WebSocket fix refuses the same class of ID.

### Prerequisites

- JDK 21 or newer (tested on Temurin/Zulu 21 and OpenJDK 25)
- Apache Maven 3.9+

